<h2>Start with what the proceeding is</h2>
<p>Section 12 lets an aggrieved person ask a Magistrate for the Act&rsquo;s reliefs. Nothing in that application accuses anyone of an offence; prosecution enters the picture only if a protection order is later breached (Section 31) or a Protection Officer&rsquo;s duty is obstructed. The Supreme Court&rsquo;s reasoning in <em>Kamatchi</em> &mdash; delivered on the limitation question &mdash; rests squarely on this architecture: the application is not a complaint, and treating it as one imports criminal-procedure doctrines the statute never invited.</p>
<p>That matters for quashing because the inherent power exists to prevent abuse of the process of the court and secure the ends of justice. Where the proceeding seeks civil protection for a woman alleging domestic violence, courts are slow to strangle it at the threshold.</p>

<h2>The grounds that work</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Ground</th><th>Why it succeeds</th></tr>
  <tr><td>No domestic relationship</td><td>The Act applies between persons who live or have lived together in a shared household in a qualifying relationship; if the pleadings themselves negate this, the foundation is absent</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Omnibus impleadment</td><td>Relatives against whom the application alleges nothing specific — married sisters-in-law living elsewhere, aged relatives abroad — can seek deletion or quashing qua themselves</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Abuse after settlement</td><td>Where monetary claims were comprehensively relinquished in a court-recorded settlement, re-agitating them through a fresh DV application has been treated as abuse of process</td></tr>
  <tr><td>No domestic violence disclosed</td><td>If every averment is taken as true and still no act within Section 3&rsquo;s definition appears, the proceeding serves no purpose</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The grounds that fail</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>&ldquo;The allegations are false.&rdquo;</strong> Truth is tried, not presumed, at the quashing stage. Save the material for cross-examination.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>&ldquo;The incidents are old.&rdquo;</strong> After <em>Kamatchi</em>, limitation under Section 468 CrPC does not bar a Section 12 application; the age of allegations goes to weight before the Magistrate, including whether a continuing wrong exists.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>&ldquo;A divorce or 498A case is already pending.&rdquo;</strong> Parallel matrimonial proceedings are the norm, not an abuse; the DV Act expressly supplements other laws (Section 36), and its reliefs can even be claimed in other pending proceedings under Section 26.</div>
</div>

<h2>Strategy for respondents in Delhi</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Audit the application first.</strong> Test it clause by clause against Sections 2(a), 2(f), 2(s) and 3 — status, relationship, household, violence. Quashing petitions succeed on definitional gaps, not indignation.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Choose the forum deliberately.</strong> For most grievances — interim maintenance quantum, procedural errors — the Section 29 appeal to the Sessions Court is faster and cheaper than a Section 528 BNSS petition to the High Court.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Preserve settlements in writing.</strong> If disputes were settled, ensure the settlement recorded the relinquishment of specific claims; that document is the backbone of any later abuse-of-process argument.</div>
</div>
<div class="note">This explainer states the general legal position for information. It is not legal advice; whether a particular DV Act proceeding can be challenged depends entirely on its pleadings and history.</div>
